<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dhana Nanda</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 321 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dhana Nanda was the last king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanda-dynasty">Nanda dynasty</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magadha">Magadha</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Greek accounts of Alexander's campaign speak of the great army of the eastern king, which helped turn Alexander's troops back at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beas">Beas</a> in 326 BCE. Indian traditions remember him as greedy, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chandragupta-maurya">Chandragupta Maurya</a> overthrew him around 322 BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dhana-nanda.jpg" alt="Dhana Nanda" width="320" height="299" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Avantiputra7 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nanda_Empire,_c.325_BCE.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>The name Dhana Nanda comes from the Buddhist text Mahabodhivamsa. The traditions disagree about his family. The Buddhist Mahāvaṃsa names nine Nanda kings, all brothers, who ruled in succession for 22 years in all, from Ugrasena to Dhana Nanda. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puranas">Puranas</a> also count nine kings but make the founder Mahapadma and the other eight his sons. Jain texts call the last king simply Nanda.</p><p>The Roman historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-curtius-rufus">Quintus Curtius Rufus</a> knew of only two kings, a barber who became king and his son. Greek accounts call Alexander's contemporary in India Agrammes or Xandrames, whom modern historians identify as the last Nanda king. Agrammes may render the Sanskrit Augrasainya, son or descendant of Ugrasena.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The Greek accounts describe Agrammes as ruler of the Gangaridai of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a> valley and of the Prasii, probably the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> prachyas, the easterners. Curtius gives his army as 200,000 infantry, 20,000 cavalry, 2,000 four-horsed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariots</a> and 3,000 elephants. All the accounts agree he was unpopular. Diodorus has <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porus">Porus</a> tell Alexander that the king was of worthless character and thought to be of low origin, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> has Androkottos, identified as Chandragupta, say later that Alexander could easily have conquered the land because its king was hated. The Sri Lankan Buddhist tradition blames the Nandas for oppressive taxation, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puranas">Puranas</a> call them adharmika, not following <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharma">dharma</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In the Buddhist legend the king insulted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chanakya">Chanakya</a> for his ugly appearance at an alms-giving at Pupphapura and had him thrown out. Chanakya cursed him, escaped with the help of the prince Pabbata, raised wealth for an army and trained Chandragupta for seven years. Their first attack on the capital was defeated; a second army took the border villages, advanced on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pataliputra">Pataliputra</a> and killed Dhana Nanda, and Chanakya made Chandragupta king.</p><p>The Jain version differs: the defeated king was allowed to leave his capital alive, and his daughter married Chandragupta. The fall of the king marked the end of the Nanda Empire and the birth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurya-empire">Maurya Empire</a>. He appears as the chief antagonist in nearly every Indian television series about <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chanakya">Chanakya</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chandragupta-maurya">Chandragupta Maurya</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dhana Nanda” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Brahmin and Buddhist sources alike remember the last king, <b>Dhana Nanda</b>, as greedy and unpopular.</blockquote><a href="/state/nanda-empire">Nanda Empire</a></li></ul></section>
